  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current algorithm uses simulated annealing with limited types of moves, which can be easily trapped in local optima. To improve, we adopt a hierarchical optimization strategy that combines a coarsely optimized genetic algorithm layer with fine-tuning. The genetic algorithm explores diverse patterns by optimizing at a multiple-of-n-points level, while the fine-tuning layer sharpens solutions using local optimization. This two-layer approach leverages both broad structural changes (population-based) and targeted refinements, enabling discovery of novel patterns that reduce maximum overlap.\n\nMathematical specification:\n1. Representation:\n   - Base solution vector h: n_points samples (initially 200 or fallback point)\n   - Coarse representation: Divide into fixed-size chunks and operate on chunk pivots\n   - Objective: Minimize max_{k} \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx \u2248 dx * max_{k} np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full').max()\n\n2. Constraints:\n   - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n   - \u2211h = n_points/2\n\n3. Objective computation:\n   - dx = 2.0/n_points\n   - corr_full = np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')\n   - C_5 = dx * np.max(corr_full)\n\n4. Algorithm components:\n   - Coarse optimization: Binary-coded chunks stored in population\n   - Fine-tuning: Gradient-based local optimization of converged solutions\n   - Hierarchical acceptance: Solutions are accepted globally only if locally improved\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - If initial_h_values available, use as base but prepare coarse population\n   - Otherwise generate random base solution and build initial population\n   - Precompute reference correlations for chunks\n\n2. Coarse population:\n   - Divide into fixed-size chunk population (size=population_size)\n   - Store only chunk endpoints to reduce dimensionality\n   - Use tournament elitism and two-point crossover\n\n3. Global selection:\n   - Select parents by tournament\n   - Apply crossover only if diversity threshold is met\n   - Maintain top-k solutions across generations\n\n4. Local optimization:\n   - Apply hierarchical gradient-based moves:\n     - Three-step gradient projections\n     - Bound-constrained updates\n   - Initialize with best population members\n\n5. Score-driven adaptation:\n   - Update chunk representations based on local optimization results\n   - Adjust population size dynamically to balance exploration/exploitation\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n1. Initial population size: 30 (can be increased for larger grids)\n2. Chunk size: 10 for 200 points (or 200/n_chunks)\n3. Local optimization iterations: 200 (time budget-dependent)\n4. Elite preservation: Keep top 5 solutions across generations\n\nNumerical robustness:\n1. All candidates must satisfy:\n   - np.all(0 <= h <= 1)\n   - np.allclose(np.sum(h), n_points/2, atol=1e-5)\n2. Fallback mechanism:\n   - When local optimization fails, revert to coarse population\n   - NaN/divergence checks in gradient computations\n   - Dynamic step size control\n\nRuntimes safeguards:\n1. Timeout enforcement through time tracking\n2. Best solution persistence across layers\n3. Early termination if score improvement stalls\n\nObjective evaluation:\n1. Use exact derviation:\n   C_5 = dx * np.max(np.concatenate([np.correlate(h,1-h)]))\n   Account for finite interval [0,2]\n   Weight as continuous integral approximation",
